  "surprise": "690 at 8 loops (seeds 687/715/669) against 675 (698/686/641): +15, about one standard error of the difference between two 3-seed means, so kept by the rule, not shown beyond noise. As predicted, 7/7/6 RL rounds fit instead of 5/5/4 and RL solved 1203 targets against 1149. The eval gap did not move: 672 at 2 loops, so 8 loops add +18 (23/19/13), the same as 500. This improves RL; it does not make loops pay more at eval. The surprise is the randomized retries. I predicted depth would stop mattering on resistant targets after round 2; it did not. Of the targets that failed 8 tries at 1 loop, pooled over seeds and rounds (about 4,100 per arm, 24 tries each), 11.4% got a proof at 2 loops, 13.8% at 4 and 15.9% at 8 (per-sample acceptance 2.3%, 3.6%, 4.7%). In rounds 3+ it is still 10.4%, 11.7%, 14.3%, and 8 loops beat 2 in every seed (67 vs 57, 99 vs 58, 121 vs 95 targets), unlike the spike's flat curve on never-solved targets. Two caveats: the retry set is selected on failing at 1 loop, which favours loop counts least like 1 loop, and an 8-loop sample costs about 2x a 2-loop one, so per GPU-second 2 loops still cracks more. Round-1 yield depends heavily on the pretrained model (1-loop acceptance 0.159/0.038/0.052 by seed).",
